Create a class Shape with a method `void area()`. Extend this class Shape to three more classes Rectangle, Triangle and Circle and override the method `area()` to compute the area of specific shape. Design a Bounded Generic class called BoundGen, which is bounded to Shape class and create a suitable constructor to instantiate object of any sub class of Shape and a `print()` method which will print area of a specific Shape. Refer the given code Snippet for your reference.

Sample Code Snippet.

```java
class Test{
public static void main(String args[]){
BoundedGen< Shape> bobj=new BoundedGen< >(new Shape());
bobj.print();//prints area of Shape
BoundedGen< Shape> sobj=new BoundedGen< >(new Triangle(2,3));
sobj.print();//prints the area of Triangle
BoundedGen< Triangle> tobj=new BoundedGen< >(new Triangle(2,3));
tobj.print();//prints the area of Triangle
BoundedGen< Rectangle> robj=new BoundedGen< >(new Rectangle(2,3));
robj.print();//Prints the area of Rectangle
}}
```

A.java and B.java are packaged in pkg1 and pkg2, respectively. The code of each files are shown below:

```java
A.java:
package pkg1;
class A{
private int x;
public int y;
protected int z;
int w;
}

B.java
package pkg2;
class B{
A a = new A();
System.out.println(a.x);
System.out.println(a.y);
System.out.println(a.z);
System.out.println(a.w);
}
```

When the files are compiled, B.java results in errors. Without changing the access modifiers of variables defined in class A, rectify the code(s) to prevent any compilation errors. Provide necessary reasons for the change(s) done.
